===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[squeeze]] [[tight]]ly into something.
#* {{RQ:Joyce Ulysses|page=481|passage=He is '''sausaged''' into several overcoats and wears a brown macintosh under which he holds a roll of parchment.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Paul Kenyon|title=I Am Justice: A Journey Out of Africa|publisher=Preface Publishing, [[w:Random House|The Random House Group]]|year=2009|page=92|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/iamjusticejourne0000keny/page/92/mode/1up|isbn=978 1 84809 145 0|passage=He leapt to his feet, carefully '''sausaged''' his screwdrivers in a roll beneath his arm and turned to reach into the box.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Michelle Dalton|title=Sixteenth Summer|publisher=Simon Pulse, {{w|Simon & Schuster}} Children’s Publishing Division|year=2011|page=90|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sixteenthsummer0000unse/page/90/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-4424-2344-2|passage=Now it was my turn to whip my wrap off the ground. I quickly '''sausaged''' myself within it while simultaneously dusting sand off my arms and legs.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Mary Elizabeth Moloney|title=Elizabeth: Learning to Dress Myself from the Inside Out|publisher=Heart Whisperings|year=2012|page=253|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9780984809707/page/253/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-9848097-0-7|passage=No longer able to wear white socks and slippers, she wore Tet Hose that '''sausaged''' her swollen feet and legs.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Katrina Onstad|title=Everybody Has Everything|publisher=Emblem, {{w|McClelland & Stewart}}|year=2012|page=116|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/everybodyhasever0000onst/page/116/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-7710-6898-0|passage=Knit blankets '''sausaged''' their legs.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Emily Perkins (novelist)|Emily Perkins]]|title=The Forrests|publisher=[[w:Bloomsbury Publishing|Bloomsbury]] Circus|year=2012|page=87|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/forrests0000perk_r2r0/page/87/mode/1up|isbn=978 1 4088 0923 5|passage=In the top drawer were neatly piled thermals and socks '''sausaged''' into pairs.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Tyrone Geronimo Johnson|title=Welcome to Braggsville|publisher=[[w:William Morrow and Company|William Morrow]]|year=2015|pages=150–151|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/welcometobraggsv0000john/page/150/mode/2up|isbn=978-0-06-230212-0|passage=Socks she '''sausaged''' like everyone else, but T-shirts she folded and stacked like a factory worker.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Caitlin Crawshaw|title=The Other F-word|journal=w:Briarpatch|volume=43|issue=2|month=March/April|year=2014|page=5|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/briarpatch-magazine-march-april-2014/page/5/mode/1up|passage=Filled to bursting with IV fluids, the skin on my hips is taut; it feels like I’ve '''sausaged''' myself into pantyhose five sizes too small.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Helen Wickes|title=World as You Left It: Poems|publisher=w:Sixteen Rivers Press|year=2015|page=59|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/worldasyouleftit0000wick/page/59/mode/1up|isbn=9781939639080|passage=Oh well, yes, I '''sausaged''' myself into the dark-blue wool—quite proper—and walked forever to the 21 Club where Dad was being tossed out—raging, whining—I hadn’t, I’ve told you already—seen him in years.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Christopher Carr|title=Mayday|edition=2nd|publisher=SynergEbooks|year=2016|page=51|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9780744323047/page/51/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-7443-2304-7|passage=Dressing in a flash, she '''sausaged''' on her skinny jeans and sleeveless camo top with peek-a-boo sides for boob aficionados.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Karen Polinsky|title=Dungeness|location=Fairfield, Calif.|publisher=Bink Books, Bedazzled Ink Publishing Company|year=2017|page=20|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dungeness0000poli/page/20/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-945805-18-9|passage=In her arms, an infant '''sausaged''' inside a rolled cedar mat.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kevin Shaw|title=Smaller Hours|publisher=Icehouse Poetry, {{w|Goose Lane Editions}}|year=2017|page=20|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/smallerhours0000shaw/page/20/mode/1up|isbn=9780864929990|passage=My soldier has pulled the maid’s apron from the mannequin in the larder, and '''sausaged''' his hairy thighs inside splitting the lace and seams, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Scrapbook: A Surcharge on the Charge, Sir|journal=w:The Weekly Standard|volume=23|issue=16|date=25 December 2017|page=2|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/the-weekly-standard-2017-12-25/page/n2/mode/1up|column=1|passage=There’s nothing worse than having cashed out the college fund so that Mom and Dad and Buddy and Sis can afford airfare to Wally World, only to find out that they are additionally facing bag fees and snack fees, and soon enough, there will likely be a fee to have the 350-pound man '''sausaged''' next to Sis in the middle seat keep his meaty elbows out of her ribcage.}}
# To make into sausage.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=M[ilburg] F[rancisco] Mansfield; [[w:Blanche McManus|B[lanche] McM[anus]]]|title=Romantic Ireland|volume=II|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Louis Coues Page|L[ouis] C[oues] Page]] & Company|year=1904|page=99|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/romanticireland02mansuoft/page/99/mode/1up|passage=There is no escaping the Limerick pig. In single file, in battalions, as solitary scout, alive or dead, baconed and '''sausaged''', he dominates the town.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Dion Fortune|title=The Sea Priestess|location=York Beach, Me.|publisher=[[w:Red Wheel/Weiser|Samuel Weiser, Inc.]]|year=1938|year_published=1989|page=245|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/seapriestess0000fort/page/245/mode/1up|isbn=0-87728-424-5|passage=I mayn’t know much about pigs, but I know a lot about Muckley, and there must be something pretty wrong with any pigs that he wouldn’t risk '''sausaging'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Landmark|publisher=Waukesha County Historical Society|year=1965|page=16|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nBErAQAAMAAJ&q=%22sausaging%22|passage=At butchering time, they kept three pigs for their own use, smoking, brining, and '''sausaging''' the meat, and trying the lard.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Susan Quinn|title=A Mind of Her Own: The Life of Karen Horney|publisher=Summit Books, [[w:Simon & Schuster|Simon & Schuster, Inc.]]|year=1987|pages=246–247|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/mindofherownlife00quinrich/page/247/mode/1up|isbn=0-671-47518-5|passage=Long afterward Renate remembered “the pigs squeaking and jerking while coming down a funnel in which they were shorn…on their way to getting quartered and '''sausaged'''” and the elevator that “released one steer at a time” to be “greeted with a blow of a sledge hammer on his head.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John James (writer)|John James]]|editors=[[w:John and Caitlin Matthews|Caitlín Matthews and John Matthews]]|title=The Fourth Gwenevere|publisher=Jo Fletcher Books, [[w:Quercus (publisher)|Quercus Editions Ltd.]]|year=a. 1994|year_published=2014|page=47|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fourthgwenevere0000jame_i3d4/page/47/mode/1up|isbn=978 1 84866 412 8|passage=The fresh meat was '''sausaged''' into the tripes of pig and deer and ox, because the time for salting was not yet come.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Leslie Land|title=High on the Hog|journal=[[w:House & Garden (magazine)|House & Garden]]|volume=164|issue=9|month=September|year=1992|page=62|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/housegarden164julnewy/page/n343/mode/1up|column=2|passage=So I took the lot and had much of it custom-smoked, then I roasted, fried, grilled, sautéed, stir-fried, stewed, braised, and '''sausaged''' the rest.}}
# To make sausage-like, especially to give the appearance of barely fitting into the casing or skin.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Jonathan Kellerman|title=[[w:Flesh and Blood (Kellerman novel)|Flesh and Blood]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Random House|year=2001|page=226|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fleshbloodnovel00kell/page/226/mode/1up|isbn=0-679-45962-6|passage=The second Mrs. Teague wore a baby blue tank top and too-tight white shorts that '''sausaged''' her hips.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Joe Schreiber|title=Eat the Dark|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Del Rey Books|Del Rey]]/{{w|Ballantine Books}}|year=2007|page=108|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/eatdarknovel0000schr/page/108/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-345-48750-6|passage=Blood and gravity had '''sausaged''' her legs and feet, fattening them into white-stocking loaves that dangled eighteen inches above her neatly folded nurse’s uniform on the floor.}}
# {{lb|en|engineering}} To form a sausage-like shape, with a non-[[uniform]] [[cross section]].

